  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The target market is not always decisive: the Manual says some trade marks are scandalous regardless of their market, particularly those promoting racial vilification, religious intolerance or personal abuse.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part30/2/2/11",
          "quote": "There are some trade marks which, no matter what the market is, will always be regarded as scandalous and therefore unacceptable to the Registrar. These trade marks are often those likely to be seen as promoting racial vilification, religious intolerance and personal abuse of an individual."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual requires the scandalous element to be obvious and up front, rather than merely a suggestion or a vague possibility of offence.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part30/2/2/4",
          "quote": "The scandalous element has to be something that is obvious and up front in the trade mark. A mere suggestion within the trade mark or a vague possibility that someone might find it offensive is not sufficient."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Context can nevertheless matter: for material of significant cultural, spiritual or religious significance to an Indigenous community, the Manual identifies the proposed goods and services as a consideration in assessing whether commercial use would be scandalous.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part30/2/2/13",
          "quote": "Where material in a trade mark is of significant cultural, spiritual or religious significance to an Indigenous community, consideration should be given as to whether the commercial use of the material in the form of a trade mark would be scandalous, including:"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part30/2/2/13",
          "quote": "The context in which the mark is proposed to be used i.e. the goods and services;"
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For Indigenous Knowledge subject matter, the Manual also allows reconsideration of a ground for rejection where information or evidence shows that the mark or its proposed use would not offend the relevant Indigenous community.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part30/2/2/13",
          "quote": "A ground for rejection may be reconsidered where information or evidence is provided showing that the trade mark or proposed use of the trade mark would not be offensive to the relevant Indigenous community."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The supplied passages do not establish a general target-market test for all offensive trade marks; they provide specific Manual guidance, and section 42(a) itself has not been supplied.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
